From: Rene Vogelaar <revogelaar@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] events: don't reflect a kernel DEL_STATION back as a disassociation

nl80211_del_station_event() turned every kernel DEL_STATION notification
into EVENT_DISASSOC toward hostapd -- including deletions hostapd itself
had just ordered.  hostapd's handle_auth() removes a stale kernel station
("start over") when a client authenticates while an old entry exists, so
the following sequence fired on every steer-back inside the
stale-association window left by a silent BTM roam-away:

    .281715  AUTH received from the returning client
    .281867  hostapd removes the STALE kernel entry     (handle_auth)
    .291536  NEW_STATION: fresh entry, added_unassoc
    .291849  auth reply, status 0 -- session has WLAN_STA_AUTH
    .292055  DEL_STATION notify of .281867 arrives  -> EVENT_DISASSOC
    .296515  hostapd_notif_disassoc -> hostapd_remove_sta:
             strips WLAN_STA_AUTH|ASSOC, sends disassoc sc:8,
             ap_free_sta() deletes the NEW kernel entry too
    .309352  client's REASSOC arrives
    .309392  rejected: deauth sc:6, class-2 from non-authenticated STA

The client then blacklists the AP and falls back, so steering a client
back to a previous AP failed on the first attempt every time (the failure
itself purges the stale entry, so a retry succeeds -- which is what made
this look intermittent).

Fresh clients never hit it: with no stale kernel entry the removal
returns -ENOENT and no notification is generated.

Upstream driver_nl80211_event.c gates this exact synthesis on
drv->device_ap_sme -- full-MAC devices whose firmware disassociates
stations autonomously -- and otherwise deliberately ignores DEL_STATION
in AP mode as the completion of hostapd's own request.  This HAL is
always userspace-SME, so drop the AP-mode synthesis entirely.  The
bridge-fdb and 4addr cleanup stay; genuine client-initiated
disassoc/deauth still arrive as management frames through
process_frame_mgmt().

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
diff --git a/wifi_hal_nl80211_events.c b/wifi_hal_nl80211_events.c
--- a/wifi_hal_nl80211_events.c
+++ b/wifi_hal_nl80211_events.c
@@ -179,7 +179,7 @@
         return;
     }
     memcpy(mac, nla_data(attr), sizeof(mac_address_t));
-    wifi_hal_error_print("%s:%d: DEL station:%s, sending event: EVENT_DISASSOC\n", __func__, __LINE__,
+    wifi_hal_dbg_print("%s:%d: DEL station:%s\n", __func__, __LINE__,
         to_mac_str(mac, mac_str));
 
     snprintf(br_buff,sizeof(br_buff),"bridge fdb del %s dev %s master",to_mac_str(mac, mac_str),interface->name); //deleting fdb entries in bridge
@@ -190,9 +190,23 @@
 #if defined(BANANA_PI_PORT) && (HOSTAPD_VERSION >= 211)
         supplicant_event(&interface->wpa_s, EVENT_DISASSOC, &event);
 #endif
-    } else {
-        wpa_supplicant_event(&interface->u.ap.hapd, EVENT_DISASSOC, &event);
     }
+    /* For an AP interface whose SME runs in userspace, a kernel DEL_STATION
+     * notification is only the completion of a removal hostapd itself
+     * requested -- reflecting it back as EVENT_DISASSOC makes hostapd tear
+     * down a session it did not lose.  handle_auth()'s clean-slate removal of
+     * a stale kernel entry then echoes back ~10 ms later and destroys the
+     * just-authenticated session, so the client's reassociation is rejected
+     * with reason 6 (class-2 frame from non-authenticated STA).  Seen every
+     * time a client is steered back to an AP inside the stale-association
+     * window after a silent BTM roam-away.
+     *
+     * Upstream driver_nl80211_event.c gates this exact synthesis on
+     * drv->device_ap_sme (full-MAC devices whose firmware disassociates
+     * stations autonomously) and otherwise ignores DEL_STATION in AP mode.
+     * This HAL is always userspace-SME, so do not synthesize the event at
+     * all.  Genuine client-initiated disassoc/deauth still arrive as
+     * management frames through process_frame_mgmt(). */
     //Remove the station from the bridge, if present
     wifi_hal_configure_sta_4addr_to_bridge(interface, 0);
 #endif
